 of the series what's  more the borough is shown fully intact  in the following film the redditch user  went on to criticize the fact that while  it was plausible the weasley family  could have repaired the house using  magic such an easy solution would render  the house's destruction in the first  place completely pointless the borough  was one of the only places that ever  truly felt like home for harry and  without any lasting repercussions within  the story the attack seems like it was  only added for flair additionally the  precious time spent on the fire also  took away from other scenes that were  needed for the film to feel satisfying  for example the only explanation for the  identity of the mysterious half-blood  prince a point so essential to the plot  it made it to the title of the book and  film installments occurs at the end when  professor snape played by alan rickman  announces it with little preamble  yes  i'm  the half blood prince  in the book the identity of the  half-blood prince holds more emotional  weight and gives context to who snape is  as a person yet it has little  consequence in the film another critique  offered by redditors was that there  should have been more time devoted to  the conversations harry and dumbledore  have about voldemort's background  additionally the inclusion of these  scenes would have made the ending of the  film more impactful without establishing  the relationship between the two  characters and showing how much time  they spent together dumbledore's death  and harry leaving school don't pack the  same punch that they should have  unfortunately this isn't the only  on-screen relationship that was cut  short perhaps the most egregious  oversight in half-blood prince was a  lack of screen time devoted to the  depiction of romantic relationships  ron and hermione take up much of the  romantic drama during the film and even  their storyline was still only a shadow  of its depiction in the book since there  was not e